---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Full-screen Workshop 1 presentation" description="Visual, interactive presenter deck for Git, GitHub, VS Code, Copilot, and GitHub Pages.">
<div class="presentation-page container">
  <div class="presentation-intro no-print">
    <div><div class="eyebrow">MIS Club · Workshop 1 · facilitator deck</div><h1>Git and GitHub: building and publishing a portfolio.</h1><p>This deck is the main facilitation tool. Teach from the yellow ask-the-room prompts, leave each slide up through the checkpoint, and open Resources (R) only when a student needs the walkthrough or a ZIP.</p></div>
    <button class="btn primary launch-fullscreen" data-fullscreen>Enter full screen ↗</button>
  </div>
  <div class="presentation-root" id="presentation-root">
    <header class="deck-topbar no-print">
      <a class="deck-brand" href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL}><span class="deck-mark">W</span><span>WICHITA STATE <small>MIS CLUB · WORKSHOP 1</small></span></a>
      <div class="deck-tools">
        <button class="deck-tool" data-toggle="resources" aria-haspopup="dialog">☷ Resources <kbd>R</kbd></button>
        <button class="deck-tool" data-toggle="notes">Presenter notes <kbd>N</kbd></button>
        <button class="deck-tool" data-fullscreen>⛶ Full screen <kbd>F</kbd></button>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div class="deck-stage" id="deck-stage" aria-live="polite">
      <section class="deck-slide work-slide cover-slide is-active" data-title="Git & GitHub: from folder to live site" data-notes="Facilitation mode: this deck is the spine. Yellow ask-the-room bars are cold-call or turn-and-talk prompts — pause and wait for answers. Leave each slide up until the checkpoint is true for most of the room. Press R only for ZIPs, the student guide, or presenter desk. Promise: local preview → one change → commit → branch/PR → live Pages URL.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>MIS CLUB WORKSHOP 01 · FIELD GUIDE</span><span>3 HOURS · HANDS-ON</span></div>
        <div class="cover-work">
          <div class="cover-copy">
            <p class="eyebrow light">WICHITA STATE UNIVERSITY</p>
            <h2>Git and GitHub:<br/><span>from your computer to</span><br/>a published site.</h2>
            <p class="cover-dek">A working guide for changing code, keeping a clean history, reviewing work, and publishing a real website.</p>
            <div class="cover-meta"><span>01 / WORKFLOW</span><span>02 / REVIEW</span><span>03 / DEPLOY</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cover-index">
            <div class="index-line"><span>01</span><b>Run the project</b><small>Local first</small></div>
            <div class="index-line"><span>02</span><b>Make one small change</b><small>One clear outcome</small></div>
            <div class="index-line"><span>03</span><b>Commit and push</b><small>Keep the history readable</small></div>
            <div class="index-line"><span>04</span><b>Review and publish</b><small>Prove it works</small></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>USE THE WALKTHROUGH WHILE YOU WORK</span><span>ARROWS TO MOVE · R FOR RESOURCES · N FOR NOTES</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="The target: one complete change" data-notes="Walk through the definition of done before students start. The target is one focused change that the student can explain, review, and publish. If someone gets stuck, use the troubleshooting slide and keep going from a working checkpoint. If students get stuck, they can use the checkpoint on the relevant slide rather than jumping ahead.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>START HERE</span><span>01 / PLAN</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">TODAY’S GOAL</p><h2>Today’s goal: make and publish one change.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">You don’t need to finish the whole portfolio today. Make one useful change, review it, and follow the steps through publishing.</p></div>
        <div class="target-grid">
          <div class="target-step"><span>01</span><h3>Run locally</h3><p>The project opens in VS Code and the local preview loads.</p></div>
          <div class="target-step"><span>02</span><h3>Change one thing</h3><p>Update one project, profile detail, or content section.</p></div>
          <div class="target-step"><span>03</span><h3>Record the work</h3><p>Check the diff, stage only intended files, and make a clear commit.</p></div>
          <div class="target-step"><span>04</span><h3>Review + publish</h3><p>Open a pull request, merge after review, then check the live URL.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="run-of-show">
          <div><b>15 min</b><span>Theme + empty repo</span></div><div><b>15 min</b><span>Open + local preview</span></div><div><b>15 min</b><span>First push (git init)</span></div><div><b>20 min</b><span>Personalize + commit</span></div><div><b>10 min</b><span>Break</span></div><div><b>30 min</b><span>Issue → branch → PR</span></div><div><b>30 min</b><span>Pages + lab</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>In one sentence: what will you be able to show someone at the end of today?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>A CHANGE YOU CAN EXPLAIN AND SHOW</span><span>FOLLOW THE STEPS IN ORDER</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Download the starter" data-notes="Pause until every student has an unzipped ZIP and (ideally) an empty public GitHub repo with no README. One starter for everyone — no theme pick. Cold-call: what folder must contain package.json after unzip? Do not start git init until local preview works.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>BEFORE CODE · STARTER</span><span>02 / DOWNLOAD</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">ONE LAYOUT FOR EVERYONE</p><h2>Download the starter ZIP. Unzip it.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Same resume site for the whole room. Empty sections stay hidden — fill what you have in <code>profile.js</code>.</p></div>
        <div class="theme-pick theme-pick-single">
          <a class="theme-card student" href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'downloads/portfolio-starter.zip'}><span>01</span><b>Resume starter</b><small>Edit profile.js · publish with Pages</small><em>Download ZIP ↓</em></a>
        </div>
        <div class="theme-pick-foot">
          <a class="docs-inline-link" href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'designs/'}>Open live preview ↗</a>
          <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>Raise your hand when your ZIP is downloaded and unzipped. What folder did you get?</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note"><b>Also now:</b> on GitHub create an empty <b>Public</b> repository (no README / license / .gitignore). You will push after the local preview works.</div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>ONE ZIP PER STUDENT</span><span>ALSO CREATE EMPTY PUBLIC REPO</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Step 1 — Open the project and run it" data-notes="Primary path is the theme ZIP (not a clone). Open Folder on the unzipped project root. Leave this slide up until localhost loads. Cold-call: what folder must contain package.json? If npm fails, read the first error.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 1 · ENVIRONMENT</span><span>03 / SETUP</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">GET A KNOWN-GOOD START</p><h2>Open the project. Run it before editing.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Start in the project root — the folder with <code>package.json</code>.</p></div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="plain-panel">
            <h3>In VS Code</h3>
            <ol class="numbered-steps"><li>Open the unzipped <code>my-resume-site</code> folder from the previous slide (File → Open Folder…).</li><li>In VS Code choose <b>File → Open Folder…</b></li><li>Select the project folder, not just one file.</li><li>Open <b>Terminal → New Terminal</b>.</li></ol>
            <div class="checkpoint"><b>Checkpoint</b><span>The Explorer shows <code>src/</code>, <code>public/</code>, and <code>package.json</code>.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cmd-panel">
            <div class="panel-label">TERMINAL · RUN IN PROJECT ROOT</div>
            <pre><code>npm install
npm run dev</code></pre>
            <div class="command-explainer"><code>npm install</code><span>Installs the packages listed by the project.</span></div>
            <div class="command-explainer"><code>npm run dev</code><span>Starts the local development server.</span></div>
            <div class="command-explainer"><code>Ctrl + C</code><span>Stops the server. On Mac use Control+C (not Command+C).</span></div>
            <div class="checkpoint"><b>Checkpoint</b><span>Open the local URL shown in the terminal, usually <code>http://localhost:4321</code>.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>Did you open the folder that contains <code>package.json</code>, or only one file? Hands up when the local preview loads.</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>RUN THE STARTER BEFORE EDITING</span><span>LOCAL PREVIEW FIRST</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Know which file to change" data-notes="Leave the Explorer diagram up and walk the room: expand src → data → profile.js. Cold-call three students to name what lives in that file. Photo goes in public/. Prefer theme in profile.js over editing layouts. After they find the file, flash Source Control later when committing — this slide is about finding content, not the diff yet.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 1 · PROJECT MAP</span><span>04 / FIND THE FILE</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">READ THE PROJECT BEFORE CHANGING IT</p><h2>Open Explorer → src → data → profile.js.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Almost all resume content lives in that one file. Photos go in <code>public/</code>.</p></div>
        <div class="file-map file-map-explorer">
          <figure class="slide-explorer" aria-label="Where to find profile.js in VS Code Explorer">
            <div class="actual-screen-bar"><span>VS CODE · EXPLORER</span><span>FIND THIS FILE</span></div>
            <div class="explorer-panel">
              <div class="explorer-sidebar" aria-hidden="true">
                <div class="explorer-title">EXPLORER</div>
                <ul class="explorer-tree">
                  <li class="folder open">portfolio-…</li>
                  <li class="indent folder open">public</li>
                  <li class="indent2 file">photo.svg</li>
                  <li class="indent folder open">src</li>
                  <li class="indent2 folder open">data</li>
                  <li class="indent3 file active">profile.js</li>
                  <li class="indent2 folder">layouts</li>
                  <li class="indent2 folder">pages</li>
                  <li class="indent file">package.json</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="explorer-copy">
                <p class="eyebrow">PATH TO OPEN</p>
                <p class="explorer-path"><code>src</code> → <code>data</code> → <code>profile.js</code></p>
                <p>Expand <b>src</b>, then <b>data</b>, then click <b>profile.js</b>. That file holds name, theme, education, coursework or experience, projects, and photo path.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <div class="file-guidance"><div class="file-callout"><span class="file-label">CONTENT TASK</span><h3>Start here</h3><code>src/data/profile.js</code><p>Update theme, bio, skills, education, experience, links, projects, and photo.</p></div><div class="file-callout"><span class="file-label">PHOTO TASK</span><h3>Optional headshot</h3><code>public/photo.jpg</code><p>Starter ships <code>photo.svg</code>. Optional: add <code>photo.jpg</code>, then set <code>photo: '/photo.jpg'</code>.</p></div><div class="file-callout"><span class="file-label">LAYOUT TASK</span><h3>Theme switch</h3><code>theme: 'emerging'</code><p>Change the theme string in profile.js before editing layout files.</p></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>Without looking: which three folders do you expand to reach the resume content file?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>EXPLORER: SRC → DATA → PROFILE.JS</span><span>ASK COPILOT TO EXPLAIN BEFORE EDITING</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="The mental model — editor, Git, GitHub, Pages" data-notes="Teach this as the spine for the rest of the session. Cold-call: if you saved in VS Code, is the live site updated? Wait for no. Then walk Save → Commit → Push → Deploy. Revisit this slide whenever someone is stuck on 'I pushed but the site didn't change.'">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>FOUNDATION</span><span>05 / THE MODEL</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">HOW A CHANGE GETS PUBLISHED</p><h2>What happens between editing and publishing?</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">The most common confusion is assuming a save, a commit, a push, and a deployment are the same thing.</p></div>
        <div class="pipeline">
          <div class="pipeline-node"><span>01</span><b>VS Code</b><p>Edit files on your computer.</p><small>SAVE</small></div><i>→</i><div class="pipeline-node"><span>02</span><b>Git</b><p>Stage and record a local snapshot.</p><small>COMMIT</small></div><i>→</i><div class="pipeline-node"><span>03</span><b>GitHub</b><p>Share commits and review the change.</p><small>PUSH / PR</small></div><i>→</i><div class="pipeline-node"><span>04</span><b>Actions</b><p>Install, build, and deploy.</p><small>WORKFLOW</small></div><i>→</i><div class="pipeline-node"><span>05</span><b>Pages</b><p>Serve the published site.</p><small>LIVE URL</small></div>
        </div>
        <div class="status-key"><div><b>Saved</b><span>The file on your computer changed.</span></div><div><b>Committed</b><span>Git recorded a snapshot in local history.</span></div><div><b>Pushed</b><span>Your commit was sent to GitHub.</span></div><div><b>Deployed</b><span>The automated publish finished; verify the URL.</span></div></div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>Raise your hand if you think saving a file in VS Code updates the live website. (It does not — wait for hands, then correct.)</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>SAVE ≠ COMMIT ≠ PUSH ≠ DEPLOY</span><span>CHECK EACH STAGE</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="First push — connect a local project to GitHub" data-notes="Students should already have an empty public repo from the theme slide (or create it now). Live-demo git init → first commit → push. Cover browser auth / PAT when push fails. Cold-call: why Public? ZIP path only — no clone.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 2 · FIRST PUSH</span><span>06 / REMOTE SETUP</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">AFTER THE LOCAL PREVIEW WORKS</p><h2>Initialize Git, then push to your empty public repo.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use the empty <b>Public</b> repository you created (no README). This is the first time <code>git init</code> runs — do it before any other Git commands.</p></div>
        <div class="push-layout">
          <figure class="actual-screen github-screen repo-screen">
            <div class="actual-screen-bar"><span>GITHUB · NEW REPOSITORY</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/creating-and-managing-repositories/creating-a-new-repository" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official source ↗</a></div>
            <img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/repository/create-repository-name.png" alt="Real GitHub create repository form showing the repository name field." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-create-repository.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" />
            <figcaption>Official GitHub Docs. Name the repo, choose <b>Public</b>, leave README / license / .gitignore unchecked.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="remote-steps compact-remote">
            <div class="remote-action"><span class="action-n">A</span><div><b>Confirm the empty public repo</b><p>If you do not have one yet: <b>New repository</b> → Public → leave README / license / .gitignore unchecked. <a class="docs-inline-link" href="https://github.com/new" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com/new ↗</a></p></div></div>
            <div class="remote-action"><span class="action-n">B</span><div><b>Initialize and first commit</b><pre><code>git init
git branch -M main
git add .
git diff --staged
git commit -m "Create my portfolio"</code></pre></div></div>
            <div class="remote-action"><span class="action-n">C</span><div><b>Add remote and push</b><pre><code>git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY.git
git remote -v
git push -u origin main</code></pre></div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="auth-panel"><b>If push asks for a password</b><span>GitHub rejects account passwords for HTTPS. Use browser sign-in when prompted, or a classic Personal Access Token with <code>repo</code> scope (paste it as the password). Do not force-push. Walkthrough: student guide Step 3.</span></div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>Why must the repository be <b>Public</b> for free GitHub Pages on a student account?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>GIT INIT STARTS HISTORY</span><span>THEN PUSH TO ORIGIN</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Git command quick reference" data-notes="Only after first push / git init. Have every student run git status. Cold-call: what four things can status tell you? Safe reads only.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 3 · INSPECT BEFORE ACTING</span><span>07 / GIT BASICS</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">COMMANDS TO KEEP HANDY</p><h2>Check the current state first.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use these after <code>git init</code> from the previous slide. Start with <code>git status</code> when you're unsure.</p></div>
        <div class="command-reference">
          <div class="command-row"><code>git status</code><div><b>What is changed?</b><span>Shows the branch, staged files, unstaged edits, and untracked files.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git diff</code><div><b>What did I edit?</b><span>Shows changes that are not staged yet.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git diff --staged</code><div><b>What will I commit?</b><span>Shows the staged snapshot that the next commit will record.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git log --oneline -5</code><div><b>What happened recently?</b><span>Shows the five latest commits in a compact list.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git branch --show-current</code><div><b>Where am I working?</b><span>Prints the current branch name.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git remote -v</code><div><b>Where does push go?</b><span>Shows the remote repository URL, normally named <code>origin</code>.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note"><b>Rule:</b> read <code>git status</code> and the diff before you stage, commit, merge, or troubleshoot.</div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>Turn and talk (30 sec): which command do you run first when something looks wrong?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>INSPECT FIRST · MAKE ONE CHANGE AT A TIME</span><span>THESE COMMANDS ONLY SHOW THE CURRENT STATE</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="The four-step commit cycle" data-notes="This is for feature commits after the first push. Live-demo once. Do not git add . for feature work. If someone still sees 'not a git repository', send them back to the first-push slide.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 3 · RECORD A CHANGE</span><span>08 / COMMIT</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">MAKING A COMMIT</p><h2>Review the diff before you commit.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use this cycle for later edits (after your first push). Keep each commit to one change.</p></div>
        <div class="commit-flow">
          <div class="commit-stage"><span>01 · READ</span><pre><code>git status
git diff</code></pre><p>See what's changed. Confirm the file and the details.</p></div>
          <div class="commit-stage"><span>02 · STAGE</span><pre><code>git add src/data/profile.js</code></pre><p>Choose the file for this commit. Replace the path if your task changed another file.</p></div>
          <div class="commit-stage"><span>03 · REVIEW</span><pre><code>git diff --staged</code></pre><p>Confirm only the intended change is staged.</p></div>
          <div class="commit-stage"><span>04 · SAVE HISTORY</span><pre><code>git commit -m "Update project details"</code></pre><p>Record the staged snapshot with a clear message.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="commit-bottom"><div><b>Good commit</b><span><code>Add featured project</code></span><span><code>Fix mobile project spacing</code></span></div><div><b>Vague commit</b><span><code>updates</code></span><span><code>stuff</code></span></div><div class="checkpoint"><b>After committing</b><span>Run <code>git status</code> again; the working tree should be clean for this task.</span></div></div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>Which message would you accept in a PR review: <code>updates</code> or <code>Add featured project</code>? Why?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>STAGE ONLY THE FILES YOU MEAN TO COMMIT</span><span>COMMIT WHAT YOU INTEND</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Branch workflow — commands and purpose" data-notes="Demo switch -c once. Cold-call: what goes wrong if you commit feature work on main? Leave the command list up while students create their branch.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 4 · SAFE, REVIEWABLE CHANGE</span><span>09 / BRANCHES</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">USE A BRANCH FOR EACH TASK</p><h2>Make the change on a separate branch, then open a pull request.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use this sequence after the first push has succeeded.</p></div>
        <div class="branch-workflow">
          <div class="branch-commands"><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git switch main
git pull</code></pre><p>Start from the latest main branch.</p></div><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git switch -c add-featured-project</code></pre><p>Create and move to a new branch.</p></div><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git status
git diff</code></pre><p>Edit the project, then inspect the change.</p></div><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git add src/data/profile.js
git diff --staged
git commit -m "Add featured project"</code></pre><p>Stage, review, and record one task.</p></div><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git push -u origin add-featured-project</code></pre><p>Publish the branch so GitHub can create a pull request.</p></div></div>
          <div class="branch-rules"><h3>Why these commands?</h3><div><code>git switch main</code><span>Move to the main branch.</span></div><div><code>git pull</code><span>Bring down remote changes before starting.</span></div><div><code>git switch -c NAME</code><span>Create a branch and switch to it.</span></div><div><code>git push -u origin NAME</code><span>Push the branch and set its upstream.</span></div><div class="checkpoint"><b>Check before editing</b><span><code>git branch --show-current</code> should print <code>add-featured-project</code>.</span></div>
          <div class="engage tight"><b>Ask</b><span>Why not commit feature work directly on <code>main</code>?</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>DON'T DO FEATURE WORK DIRECTLY ON MAIN</span><span>BRANCH → COMMIT → PUSH → PR</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Recover safely — common undo commands" data-notes="Emphasize the difference between unstaged edits, staged changes, and committed work. The first two recovery commands shown here are scoped to one file and are useful for common mistakes. git restore FILE discards local edits to that file, so first make sure the edits are not needed. Avoid git reset --hard and force pushes in the workshop; ask for help rather than risking lost work.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 4 · RECOVERY</span><span>10 / UNDO SAFELY</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">HOW TO UNDO A CHANGE</p><h2>Check what changed before you undo anything.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use the least destructive action that matches the state of your file.</p></div>
        <div class="recovery-table">
          <div class="recovery-head"><span>Situation</span><span>Command</span><span>What it does</span></div>
          <div class="recovery-row"><b>Unstage one file</b><code>git restore --staged src/data/profile.js</code><span>Removes it from the next commit; keeps your edits in the file.</span></div>
          <div class="recovery-row"><b>Discard edits to one file</b><code>git restore src/data/profile.js</code><span>Replaces that file with the last committed version. Your edits to it are lost.</span></div>
          <div class="recovery-row"><b>See the last commits</b><code>git log --oneline -5</code><span>Inspect history before choosing a recovery step.</span></div>
          <div class="recovery-row"><b>Confused or conflict shown</b><code>git status</code><span>Read the state. Stop and ask before running more commands.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="safety-ribbon"><b>Do not use these as a guess:</b><code>git reset --hard</code><code>git push --force</code><span>They can discard work or overwrite shared history.</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>IF YOU’RE UNSURE, STOP AND CHECK STATUS</span><span>STATUS FIRST · ASK IF UNSURE</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Create a useful issue before coding" data-notes="Show the New issue screenshot. Students invent one featured-project issue with checkable criteria. Cold-call: could a stranger verify each criterion without reading your mind?">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 5 · DEFINE THE TASK</span><span>11 / ISSUE</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">CREATE AN ISSUE FIRST</p><h2>Describe the change and how you’ll check it.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Create an issue in GitHub: <b>Issues → New issue</b>. Use a clear title and acceptance criteria.</p></div>
        <div class="issue-work">
          <figure class="actual-screen github-screen">
            <div class="actual-screen-bar"><span>GITHUB · NEW ISSUE</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/creating-an-issue" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official source ↗</a></div>
            <img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/issues/issue-title-body.png" alt="Real GitHub new issue form with the title and body fields filled in." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-issue.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" />
            <figcaption>Official GitHub Docs screenshot of the real issue title and body fields. Add a goal and checkable acceptance criteria.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="issue-instructions"><h3>On GitHub</h3><ol class="numbered-steps"><li>Open your repository.</li><li>Select <b>Issues → New issue</b>.</li><li>Choose a template or open a blank issue.</li><li>Use the title and acceptance criteria below.</li><li>Create the issue before changing code.</li></ol><div class="issue-criteria-example"><b>Acceptance criteria</b><span>Honest project summary · my contribution · working link · readable on mobile · no secrets</span></div><div class="checkpoint"><b>Checkpoint</b><span>Could someone who didn't write the code verify each criterion? If not, make it more specific.</span></div>
          <div class="engage tight"><b>Ask</b><span>Read a neighbor's criteria. Could you verify their done state?</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>CREATE AN ISSUE BEFORE CODING</span><span>REVIEW THE RESULT AGAINST THE CRITERIA</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Use Copilot with a reviewable prompt" data-notes="Project Pass 1 on the screen; wait until students open the named files. Then Pass 2 with their real project. Cold-call: what would you refuse to paste into Copilot? Stress review responsibility.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 6 · AI-ASSISTED EDITING</span><span>12 / COPILOT</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT TO INCLUDE IN A COPILOT REQUEST</p><h2>Ask Copilot to make one specific change.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use Copilot Chat in VS Code. Don't ask it to “make the whole site better.”</p></div>
        <div class="prompt-workspace">
          <div class="prompt-workflow">
          <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-head"><span>PASS 1</span><b>Understand first — no edits</b></div><pre><code>Inspect this Astro portfolio project. Do not edit any files yet.
Tell me which file contains the profile and project content,
how the projects are rendered, and which command builds the site.
Keep the explanation beginner-friendly and name the exact files.</code></pre><p>Read the answer. Open the files it names and confirm the explanation.</p></div>
          <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-head"><span>PASS 2</span><b>Request one focused edit</b></div><pre><code>In src/data/profile.js, update the project content for this task:
[describe my real project and my contribution].
Keep the existing data structure and visual design.
Do not add dependencies or edit other files.
If the current structure is unclear, ask before changing it.
After the edit, explain every change and how I can verify it.</code></pre><p>Replace the bracketed instruction with truthful content. Review each file changed.</p></div>
        </div>
          <figure class="actual-screen copilot-screen">
            <div class="actual-screen-bar"><span>VS CODE · COPILOT CHAT</span><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/chat/copilot-chat-context" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official source ↗</a></div>
            <img src="https://code.visualstudio.com/assets/docs/chat/copilot-chat/copilot-chat-view-chat-variables.png" alt="Real VS Code Copilot Chat interface showing workspace context suggestions in the chat input." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/copilot-prompt.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" />
            <figcaption>Official VS Code screenshot. Add the project/file context, then ask for one focused task and review the proposed edits.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="ai-rules"><div><b>Don’t paste</b><span>Passwords, API keys, private keys, tokens, or sensitive personal data.</span></div><div><b>Check the result</b><span>Generated code, claims, links, and accessibility can all be wrong.</span></div><div><b>Know what changed</b><span>You should be able to explain and maintain the code you submit.</span></div></div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>What is one thing you would refuse to paste into Copilot Chat?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>COPILOT SUGGESTS CHANGES. YOU REVIEW THEM.</span><span>DESCRIBE ONE TASK PER REQUEST</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Review the diff and run the build" data-notes="Gate before PR. Have students shout out when build succeeds. Cold-call one student to explain their diff in one sentence.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 7 · VERIFY BEFORE REVIEW</span><span>13 / QUALITY CHECK</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">CHECK THE RESULT BEFORE OPENING A PULL REQUEST</p><h2>Read the diff and test your change before asking for review.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use the same checks every time, even when the code was written with AI assistance.</p></div>
        <div class="verify-grid">
          <div class="verify-terminal"><div class="panel-label">TERMINAL · PROJECT ROOT</div><pre><code>git status
git diff --check
git diff
npm run build</code></pre><div class="verify-result"><b>Expected result</b><span>Build completes without an error. Diff contains only the intended change.</span></div></div>
          <div class="review-checklist"><h3>Human review checklist</h3><label><i>□</i><span>Every changed file is needed for this task.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>I can explain the changes line by line at a high level.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>No invented facts, credentials, tokens, or private data.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Links, headings, keyboard focus, and mobile layout work.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Acceptance criteria are met in the local browser.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>The workflow or configuration wasn't changed unnecessarily.</span></label><div class="checkpoint"><b>If a check fails</b><span>Fix one cause, rerun the failed check, and inspect the new diff.</span></div>
          <div class="engage tight"><b>Ask</b><span>Can you explain your diff in one sentence before opening a PR?</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>TEST LOCALLY BEFORE PUSHING</span><span>FIX THE SMALLEST CAUSE</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Open and review a pull request" data-notes="Switch to browser. Confirm base/compare. Have partners review Files changed. Cold-call: what would make you reject this PR? Merge only when criteria pass.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 8 · REVIEW ON GITHUB</span><span>14 / PULL REQUEST</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">REVIEW THE CHANGE ON GITHUB</p><h2>Check the diff and acceptance criteria before merging.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">After <code>git push -u origin add-featured-project</code>, open GitHub in your browser.</p></div>
        <div class="pr-workflow">
          <div class="pr-steps"><div><span>1</span><p>Open the repo and select <b>Compare &amp; pull request</b>, or go to <b>Pull requests → New pull request</b>.</p></div><div><span>2</span><p>Set <b>base: main</b> and <b>compare: add-featured-project</b>.</p></div><div><span>3</span><p>Enter a clear title and summarize the change, tests, and any limitations.</p></div><div><span>4</span><p>Open <b>Files changed</b>. Read the diff and check the issue criteria.</p></div><div><span>5</span><p>Merge only when the change is correct. Confirm the branch update in the repository.</p></div></div>
          <figure class="actual-screen github-screen pr-screen">
            <div class="actual-screen-bar"><span>GITHUB · PULL REQUEST REVIEW</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/how-tos/review-pull-requests/reviewing-proposed-changes-in-a-pull-request" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official source ↗</a></div>
            <img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pull_requests/diff-settings-menu.png" alt="Real GitHub pull request Files changed view showing the diff display settings." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-pull-request.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" />
            <figcaption>Official GitHub Docs screenshot of the real Files changed review interface. Read the diff and check the acceptance criteria before merging.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note"><b>PR rule:</b> describe what changed, how you checked it, and any risks. “Copilot wrote it” is not a review summary.</div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>What is one reason you would <b>not</b> merge a classmate’s pull request yet?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>BRANCH → PULL REQUEST → REVIEW → MERGE</span><span>CHECK THE FILES CHANGED TAB</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Publish with GitHub Pages and Actions" data-notes="Show Source = GitHub Actions, then Visit site. Stress merge to main. First run often fails until Source is set — teach Re-run all jobs. Cold-call: does a feature branch publish? Wait for no.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 9 · DEPLOYMENT</span><span>15 / PAGES + ACTIONS</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">PUBLISH THE BUILT SITE</p><h2>Merge to main → Actions → open the live URL.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Set Pages source to <b>GitHub Actions</b>. A green workflow is not enough — open the real URL.</p></div>
        <div class="deploy-workflow">
          <div class="deploy-step"><span>01</span><b>Merge to main</b><p>Reviewed PR lands on <code>main</code>.</p></div><i>→</i><div class="deploy-step dark-step"><span>02</span><b>Actions runs</b><p><code>npm install</code><br/><code>npm run build</code><br/>Uploads <code>dist/</code></p></div><i>→</i><div class="deploy-step"><span>03</span><b>Pages deploys</b><p>Static output goes live.</p></div><i>→</i><div class="deploy-step"><span>04</span><b>Verify URL</b><p>Test content, links, assets.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pages-settings pages-settings-shots">
          <figure class="actual-screen pages-screen">
            <div class="actual-screen-bar"><span>SETTINGS → PAGES · SOURCE</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/configuring-a-publishing-source-for-your-github-pages-site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official source ↗</a></div>
            <img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pages/publishing-source-drop-down.png" alt="Real GitHub Pages settings showing the publishing source dropdown." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-pages.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" />
            <figcaption>Choose <b>GitHub Actions</b> under Build and deployment.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="actual-screen pages-screen">
            <div class="actual-screen-bar"><span>PAGES · PUBLISHED URL</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/configuring-a-publishing-source-for-your-github-pages-site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official source ↗</a></div>
            <img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pages/click-pages-url-to-preview.png" alt="Real GitHub Pages settings showing the published site URL." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-pages.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" />
            <figcaption>After a green Actions run, open <b>Visit site</b>.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="pages-check-list">
            <b>Check the run</b>
            <ol><li>Open <b>Actions</b>.</li><li>Open the latest run.</li><li>Confirm success or read the first failed step.</li><li>Open the deployed URL and test it.</li></ol>
            <div class="engage tight"><b>Ask</b><span>Does pushing a feature branch publish the live site?</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note warning-note"><b>Important:</b> merge the reviewed PR into <code>main</code>, then wait for Actions. Feature branches do not publish this starter.</div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>MAIN → ACTIONS → PAGES</span><span>TEST THE REAL URL</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Troubleshooting — start with the evidence" data-notes="Use this as the help desk slide during lab. Point at the Actions log screenshot. Protocol: status → reproduce → first error → one fix. Cold-call a stuck student to narrate their symptom without fixing yet.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 10 · DEBUG</span><span>16 / TROUBLESHOOTING</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">FIND THE STEP THAT FAILED</p><h2>Find the failing step before changing anything.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Start with the symptom, inspect evidence, and change one thing at a time.</p></div>
        <div class="troubleshooting-layout">
          <div class="troubleshooting-list">
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>“not a git repository”</b><span>Run <code>pwd</code> / inspect the folder. Move to the project root that contains <code>package.json</code>; use <code>git init</code> only for a downloaded ZIP without Git.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>Push asks for password / auth failed</b><span>Use browser sign-in, or a classic PAT with <code>repo</code> scope as the password — not the GitHub account password. See student guide Step 3.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>Push rejected</b><span>Run <code>git status</code> and <code>git remote -v</code>. Stop and inspect why the remote differs; don't force push or blindly overwrite history.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>Build failed in Actions</b><span>Open the failed job log and find the first useful error. Reproduce locally with <code>npm run build</code>; fix the referenced file and push again.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>First Actions run failed</b><span>Confirm Settings → Pages → Source = <b>GitHub Actions</b>, then open the failed run and choose <b>Re-run all jobs</b>.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>Site URL returns 404</b><span>Check Settings → Pages, confirm the latest workflow succeeded, and use the full project URL including the repository path.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>HTML loads, styles/assets don't</b><span>Check the asset URL in browser dev tools and the Astro base path. A leading slash can point at the domain root rather than the project subpath.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>Latest change isn't visible</b><span>Confirm the PR was merged to <code>main</code>, the newest Actions run succeeded, the URL is correct, then hard refresh.</span></div>
        </div>
          <figure class="actual-screen actions-screen">
            <div class="actual-screen-bar"><span>GITHUB ACTIONS · JOB LOGS</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/monitor-workflows/use-workflow-run-logs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official source ↗</a></div>
            <img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/repository/actions-quickstart-log-detail.png" alt="Real GitHub Actions job log with an expanded workflow step and its output." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-actions-run.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" />
            <figcaption>Official GitHub Docs screenshot. Open the failed job, expand the failed step, and read the first useful error.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note"><b>Useful diagnostic order:</b> <code>git status</code> → reproduce locally → read the error message → make the smallest fix → rerun the check.</div>
        <div class="engage"><b>Ask the room</b><span>Before changing anything: what is the exact error text or screenshot you would show a helper?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>READ THE FIRST ERROR BEFORE CHANGING FILES</span><span>CHANGE ONE THING, THEN TEST AGAIN</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Hands-on lab — implement the issue" data-notes="Leave this slide up for ~30 min. Circulate: ask each student to show issue, branch name, and git status before debugging for them. Prefer a finished small change.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>LAB · BUILD</span><span>17 / WORK ALONG</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">PRACTICE · 30 MINUTES</p><h2>Implement the featured project issue.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Keep this slide up while you work. Complete the steps in order; don't skip the review.</p></div>
        <div class="lab-layout">
          <div class="lab-checklist"><div class="lab-title">WORK CHECKLIST</div><label><i>□</i><span>Issue exists with testable acceptance criteria.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Working branch is <code>add-featured-project</code>.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>One real project is added in <code>src/data/profile.js</code>.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Local preview shows the project and its link works.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span><code>git diff</code> shows only the intended files.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span><code>npm run build</code> completes.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Commit message explains the change.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Feature branch is pushed; PR is open.</span></label></div>
          <div class="lab-steps"><div><span>1</span><b>Create the branch</b><code>git switch main</code><code>git pull</code><code>git switch -c add-featured-project</code></div><div><span>2</span><b>Edit + inspect</b><p>Update the project data. Run <code>npm run dev</code>, check desktop and mobile, then inspect <code>git diff</code>.</p></div><div><span>3</span><b>Commit + push</b><code>git add src/data/profile.js</code><code>git diff --staged</code><code>git commit -m "Add featured project"</code><code>git push -u origin add-featured-project</code></div><div><span>4</span><b>Open the PR</b><p>Link or reference the issue, list checks performed, review Files changed, then merge when ready.</p></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>WHEN ASKING FOR HELP, SHOW THE ERROR AND GIT STATUS</span><span>SHOW YOUR STATUS + DIFF</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Lab checkpoint — explain the change" data-notes="Run this as a gallery walk or pair share. Each student shows issue → diff → PR → URL (or Actions). Instructor check: explain one Copilot line and one verification.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>LAB · REVIEW</span><span>18 / CHECKPOINT</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">SHOW YOUR WORK</p><h2>Show what you changed and how you tested it.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">You’re done when you can show the issue, the code change, the pull request, and the published page.</p></div>
        <div class="checkpoint-grid">
          <div><span>01</span><h3>Task</h3><p>Which issue did you implement? How does the result meet each criterion?</p><b>Show the issue.</b></div>
          <div><span>02</span><h3>Code</h3><p>Which file changed? Why was it the right file? What does the diff show?</p><b>Show the file + diff.</b></div>
          <div><span>03</span><h3>History</h3><p>What branch and commit hold the change? Why is the message useful?</p><b>Show <code>git log</code>.</b></div>
          <div><span>04</span><h3>Review</h3><p>What did you test? What review feedback or acceptance check matters most?</p><b>Show the PR.</b></div>
          <div><span>05</span><h3>Deployment</h3><p>Did Actions succeed? Have you checked the actual published page, not just local preview?</p><b>Show Actions + URL.</b></div>
        </div>
        <div class="checkpoint"><b>Instructor check</b><span>Ask the student to explain one line changed by Copilot and one check they used to verify it.</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>SHOW THE ISSUE, DIFF, PULL REQUEST, AND LIVE URL</span><span>REVIEWED AND TESTED</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Keep-this-open Git cheat sheet" data-notes="Park this slide during leftover lab time. Exit ticket aloud. Resources (R) for ZIPs and student guide. Remind: status first, verify live URL.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>QUICK REFERENCE</span><span>19 / CHEAT SHEET</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">COMMANDS TO KEEP HANDY</p><h2>Run these commands and read the output.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">This is the compact sequence to leave on screen during independent work.</p></div>
        <div class="cheat-columns">
          <div class="cheat-section"><h3>Inspect</h3><pre><code>git status
git diff
git diff --staged
git log --oneline -5
git branch --show-current</code></pre></div>
          <div class="cheat-section"><h3>Record</h3><pre><code>git add path/to/file
git diff --staged
git commit -m "Describe the change"
git status</code></pre></div>
          <div class="cheat-section"><h3>Branch + share</h3><pre><code>git switch main
git pull
git switch -c feature/name
git push -u origin feature/name</code></pre></div>
          <div class="cheat-section"><h3>Validate + publish</h3><pre><code>npm run dev
npm run build
# push branch → open PR
# merge → check Actions
# open and test live URL</code></pre></div>
        </div>
        <div class="exit-ticket"><b>EXIT TICKET</b><span>What changed? Where is the diff? What proves it works? What happens after merge?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>STATUS FIRST · DIFF ALWAYS · VERIFY THE LIVE URL</span><span>RESOURCES: PRESS R</span></div>
      </section>
    </div>
